1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,377
The pull request changed one line. The
page lists four commits and three files.

2
00:00:09,417 --> 00:00:14,543
The author says "I only changed one line".
The reviewer says "then why am I looking

3
00:00:14,583 --> 00:00:17,406
at three files?" Your
CTO asks: which is true?

4
00:00:17,746 --> 00:00:22,108
Both. The author is describing a
commit. The page is describing a range.

5
00:00:22,148 --> 00:00:25,594
And the range starts somewhere
the author did not expect.

6
00:00:25,934 --> 00:00:30,537
By the end of this video you will be able
to take any pull request that shows too

7
00:00:30,577 --> 00:00:35,181
much, name the cause from a short documented
list, prove it with one Git command,

8
00:00:35,221 --> 00:00:39,939
and choose the repair that fits. One question
does most of the work: where does the

9
00:00:39,979 --> 00:00:40,627
range start?

10
00:00:42,467 --> 00:00:46,299
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:46,339 --> 00:00:50,870
Two videos ago you learned the sentence
that the whole chapter hangs on.

12
00:00:50,910 --> 00:00:55,631
A pull request, a proposal to merge a
head branch into a base branch, shows

13
00:00:55,671 --> 00:00:59,313
base..head in its commit list
and base...head in its diff.

14
00:00:59,353 --> 00:01:04,519
And everything is computed from three
commits: the tip of the base, the tip of the

15
00:01:04,559 --> 00:01:09,534
head, and their merge base, the most
recent commit that both histories contain.

16
00:01:09,874 --> 00:01:15,054
So every surprise on a pull request page
is a surprise about one of those three.

17
00:01:15,094 --> 00:01:20,338
Either the head contains commits that the
base does not reach, or the base is not

18
00:01:20,378 --> 00:01:25,623
the branch the work started from. That is
section 17.12 in one sentence, and this

19
00:01:25,663 --> 00:01:27,058
video is that section.

20
00:01:27,398 --> 00:01:34,967
GitHub documents five causes. The first two
can be reproduced in plain Git, and they

21
00:01:35,007 --> 00:01:41,761
are the two replays: labs/ch17/wrong-base.sh
and labs/ch17/squash-reuse.sh.

22
00:01:41,801 --> 00:01:47,468
Then a walkthrough following Lab
21.2 on a practice repository.

23
00:01:47,808 --> 00:01:52,911
Labels: git rebase --onto is 🟡 CAUTION:
it rewrites the current branch.

24
00:01:52,951 --> 00:01:58,778
Preview with git log <upstream>..HEAD.
Recover with ORIG_HEAD, the reflog, or git

25
00:01:58,818 --> 00:02:04,863
rebase --abort. Republishing a rewritten
branch needs git push --force-with-lease, 🔴

26
00:02:04,903 --> 00:02:10,875
DANGEROUS, with the five answers from the
last video: appropriate for your own pull

27
00:02:10,915 --> 00:02:16,815
request branch after a rebase. gh pr edit
--base is 🟡: it changes the base and can

28
00:02:16,855 --> 00:02:18,119
dismiss approvals.

29
00:02:19,959 --> 00:02:21,371
After this video you can:

30
00:02:21,711 --> 00:02:26,333
Give the documented causes of a pull request
with many unrelated commits or files.

31
00:02:26,673 --> 00:02:31,248
Diagnose a pull request opened against
the wrong base and fix it by changing the

32
00:02:31,288 --> 00:02:33,325
base or by transplanting the branch.

33
00:02:33,665 --> 00:02:38,540
Explain why a branch reused after a squash
merge lists its old commits again and may

34
00:02:38,580 --> 00:02:39,067
conflict.

35
00:02:39,407 --> 00:02:42,760
Repair the reused branch
with git rebase --onto.

36
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:45,624
Name the Git command
that tests each cause.

37
00:02:47,464 --> 00:02:52,131
Cause one: the head branch was reused after
a squash merge, the merge that replaces

38
00:02:52,171 --> 00:02:56,839
your commits by one new commit. You see
commits that were "already squashed" listed

39
00:02:56,879 --> 00:03:01,490
again, and repeated conflicts. The mechanism:
the squash commit has no parent link

40
00:03:01,530 --> 00:03:04,042
to the branch, so the
merge base never moved.

41
00:03:04,382 --> 00:03:08,985
Cause two: a wrong or changed base branch.
You see every commit of the branch the

42
00:03:09,025 --> 00:03:13,801
work was really based on. The mechanism:
the range is taken against a base that does

43
00:03:13,841 --> 00:03:15,291
not contain those commits.

44
00:03:15,631 --> 00:03:20,183
Cause three: the base branch moved. The
pull request page and a compare page

45
00:03:20,223 --> 00:03:25,018
disagree, because they "can calculate
changed files from different merge bases".

46
00:03:25,058 --> 00:03:28,583
Nothing is wrong. If the two
must agree, update the branch.

47
00:03:28,923 --> 00:03:33,976
Cause four: rewritten or force-pushed history.
You see old and new copies of commits

48
00:03:34,016 --> 00:03:38,827
and outdated review comments. GitHub's
words: "Force pushing rewrites repository

49
00:03:38,867 --> 00:03:43,678
history and can ... corrupt pull requests."
And if someone force-pushes the base

50
00:03:43,718 --> 00:03:47,862
branch, every open pull request
against it lists the removed commits.

51
00:03:47,902 --> 00:03:51,379
That is a reason to block force
pushes on shared branches.

52
00:03:51,719 --> 00:03:57,142
Cause five: the diff is truncated or hidden.
Files are missing from "Files changed"

53
00:03:57,182 --> 00:04:02,079
because of display limits, or because
a .gitattributes rule hides the file.

54
00:04:02,119 --> 00:04:07,344
The file changed and the page does not
render it. git diff --stat base...head on

55
00:04:07,384 --> 00:04:09,385
your machine has no such limit.

56
00:04:09,725 --> 00:04:12,090
Now the two causes that
deserve a mechanism.

57
00:04:12,430 --> 00:04:17,577
The wrong base. On screen is today's example:
a branch created from main, for a fix

58
00:04:17,617 --> 00:04:22,765
meant for a release branch. Why does it
happen? Because people create branches from

59
00:04:22,805 --> 00:04:27,827
main by habit, and open the pull request
against wherever the fix is meant to go.

60
00:04:27,867 --> 00:04:32,827
If those are two different branches, the
range base..head contains everything on

61
00:04:32,867 --> 00:04:38,015
main that the release branch does not have.
Merging such a pull request would carry

62
00:04:38,055 --> 00:04:40,515
unreleased work into the release branch.

63
00:04:40,855 --> 00:04:44,108
There are two repairs, and
they are not interchangeable.

64
00:04:44,148 --> 00:04:47,519
If the base is wrong, change
the base of the pull request.

65
00:04:47,559 --> 00:04:52,400
GitHub warns about the side effect: "some
commits may be removed from the timeline.

66
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:56,988
Review comments may also become outdated."
If the base is right and the branch

67
00:04:57,028 --> 00:05:01,340
started in the wrong place, transplant
the commits with git rebase --onto.

68
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:06,103
A plain git rebase onto the release branch
would replay every commit, the foreign

69
00:05:06,143 --> 00:05:10,749
ones included, and change nothing about
the pull request except the commit IDs.

70
00:05:11,089 --> 00:05:15,311
Quick quiz. In our picture the
fix really is for the release.

71
00:05:15,351 --> 00:05:19,433
Which repair? A: change the
base. B: transplant the branch.

72
00:05:19,473 --> 00:05:20,271
Your answer?

73
00:05:25,111 --> 00:05:28,921
B. The base is right, and the
branch started in the wrong place.

74
00:05:28,961 --> 00:05:31,508
So the one commit moves,
and gets a new ID.

75
00:05:31,848 --> 00:05:36,562
Squash, then reuse. On screen: main after
a squash merge, and the branch that was

76
00:05:36,602 --> 00:05:41,374
squashed. This one needs the internals of
the merge base, which you learned in the

77
00:05:41,414 --> 00:05:44,778
merge videos. The merge base
comes from parent links only.

78
00:05:44,818 --> 00:05:48,828
Git never compares patches to guess
that a commit "is already there".

79
00:05:49,168 --> 00:05:54,010
A squash merge creates one new commit on
the base that copies the content of the

80
00:05:54,050 --> 00:05:59,135
branch and records no link to it. So after
the squash, for Git, the branch's commits

81
00:05:59,175 --> 00:06:04,139
were never merged. The merge base of main
and the old branch is still the original

82
00:06:04,179 --> 00:06:04,810
fork point.

83
00:06:05,150 --> 00:06:08,154
Keep working on that branch,
and two things follow.

84
00:06:08,194 --> 00:06:13,108
The range base..head lists the old commits
again. And the three-way merge sees that

85
00:06:13,148 --> 00:06:17,824
both sides added the same files since the
fork point, with different content: a

86
00:06:17,864 --> 00:06:20,510
conflict in a file that
only you ever edited.

87
00:06:20,850 --> 00:06:25,723
GitHub's page says the same: "If you keep
working on the same head branch after a

88
00:06:25,763 --> 00:06:30,576
squash merge, later pull requests can
include commits that were already squashed

89
00:06:30,616 --> 00:06:31,911
into the base branch."

90
00:06:32,251 --> 00:06:38,116
Three ways out. One: move only what is new,
with git rebase --onto origin/main <last

91
00:06:38,156 --> 00:06:43,951
squashed commit>. Two: merge main into the
branch and resolve once, which moves the

92
00:06:43,991 --> 00:06:49,646
merge base. Three, the prevention: a new
branch from main for every pull request,

93
00:06:49,686 --> 00:06:53,864
and automatic deletion of head
branches, set by gh repo edit

94
00:06:53,904 --> 00:06:55,621
--delete-branch-on-merge.

95
00:06:55,961 --> 00:07:00,314
When not to transplant: when other
people have based work on the branch.

96
00:07:00,354 --> 00:07:03,914
A rebase replaces its commits,
and the push must be forced.

97
00:07:05,754 --> 00:07:10,428
Think of the page as a ruler laid between
two points, the merge base and the head.

98
00:07:10,468 --> 00:07:15,257
Everything between the points is shown. When
the page shows too much, don't stare at

99
00:07:15,297 --> 00:07:18,361
what is shown. Ask where the
left end of the ruler is.

100
00:07:18,701 --> 00:07:23,177
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper:
draw a branch that was squashed and then

101
00:07:23,217 --> 00:07:26,130
reused, and mark where the
ruler starts. I'll wait.

102
00:07:30,970 --> 00:07:35,330
git merge-base base head tells you. If
the left end is further back than you

103
00:07:35,370 --> 00:07:40,078
expected, there are only two reasons. The
head carries history that the base never

104
00:07:40,118 --> 00:07:44,767
received as history: that is squash-then-reuse,
where the content arrived and the

105
00:07:44,807 --> 00:07:49,052
ancestry did not. Or you are measuring
against the wrong base: that is the

106
00:07:49,092 --> 00:07:49,978
wrong-base case.

107
00:07:50,318 --> 00:07:54,761
The model breaks for the fifth cause.
Truncated and hidden diffs are a property of

108
00:07:54,801 --> 00:07:59,025
the page, not of the range. There the
ruler is right and the display is short.

109
00:08:00,865 --> 00:08:05,902
Read the root-cause line: content merged
without ancestry, then more work on top of

110
00:08:05,942 --> 00:08:06,942
the old ancestry.

111
00:08:07,282 --> 00:08:11,357
The top line is main. Its tip
1b2b8ed is the squash commit.

112
00:08:11,397 --> 00:08:16,868
Look at its parents: one, 9aa221a. There
is no line from it down to the branch.

113
00:08:17,208 --> 00:08:22,064
The lower line is the branch: the three
commits of pull request 1, still there, and

114
00:08:22,104 --> 00:08:23,362
one new commit on top.

115
00:08:23,702 --> 00:08:27,418
Now lay the ruler. The merge base
is 9a383e5, at the far left.

116
00:08:27,458 --> 00:08:32,022
Between it and the head are four commits.
That's the question from the hook,

117
00:08:32,062 --> 00:08:35,900
answered: the range starts further
back than the author thought.

118
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:40,415
And the repair: the one new commit,
replayed on main, with a new ID.

119
00:08:44,755 --> 00:08:48,933
Ravi created the branch
fix/empty-subject from main, by habit.

120
00:08:48,973 --> 00:08:54,376
The fix is meant for the release branch,
so he opened the pull request with base

121
00:08:54,416 --> 00:08:55,193
release/1.0.

122
00:08:55,533 --> 00:09:00,261
One commit of his own, a1ae0eb, on top of
two commits that are on main and not on

123
00:09:00,301 --> 00:09:01,379
the release branch.

124
00:09:03,719 --> 00:09:08,815
One commit of his own. What will the
pull request against release/1.0 list?

125
00:09:09,155 --> 00:09:14,042
Three commits and three files for a one-line
fix. The two extra commits are main's.

126
00:09:14,082 --> 00:09:18,851
In a real repository this is the pull
request with hundreds of unrelated changes.

127
00:09:19,191 --> 00:09:23,155
The diagnosis is one question:
which base makes this branch small?

128
00:09:23,195 --> 00:09:27,281
Predict the two counts, against main
and against the release branch.

129
00:09:27,321 --> 00:09:28,374
Say them out loud.

130
00:09:33,214 --> 00:09:36,624
One commit against main, three
against the release branch.

131
00:09:36,664 --> 00:09:40,491
And the two merge bases show where
the ruler starts in each case.

132
00:09:40,831 --> 00:09:45,305
Fix A: the change belongs on main
after all. Same branch, other base.

133
00:09:45,645 --> 00:09:50,061
On GitHub that is gh pr edit --base
main. Nothing in Git changes.

134
00:09:50,101 --> 00:09:55,681
The pull request object gets a different
base, and the same branch is a one-commit

135
00:09:55,721 --> 00:09:56,573
pull request.

136
00:09:56,913 --> 00:10:02,137
Fix B: the change does belong on
release/1.0. Move the one commit there.

137
00:10:04,477 --> 00:10:09,125
Read the command aloud before I run it.
Which commits does it take, and where does

138
00:10:09,165 --> 00:10:09,811
it put them?

139
00:10:10,151 --> 00:10:16,080
"Take the commits of fix/empty-subject that
are not on origin/main and replay them

140
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:17,754
on origin/release/1.0."

141
00:10:18,094 --> 00:10:20,404
One commit, with a new ID, one file.

142
00:10:20,744 --> 00:10:25,169
The branch was rewritten, so
republishing it is a forced push, with

143
00:10:25,209 --> 00:10:28,767
--force-with-lease, on Ravi's
own pull request branch.

144
00:10:31,607 --> 00:10:36,771
The branch of pull request 1 is still here:
it was squash-merged, and nobody deleted

145
00:10:36,811 --> 00:10:36,957
it.

146
00:10:37,297 --> 00:10:42,324
The squash commit 1b2b8ed is on main. The
three original commits are still under the

147
00:10:42,364 --> 00:10:45,220
branch, and no line connects
them to the squash.

148
00:10:50,060 --> 00:10:54,978
You make one small follow-up commit on the
old branch, push, and open pull request

149
00:10:55,018 --> 00:10:55,099
2.

150
00:10:57,439 --> 00:11:01,853
One new commit since the squash. How
many commits will pull request 2 list?

151
00:11:02,193 --> 00:11:04,885
Four commits and two files
for a one-line change.

152
00:11:05,225 --> 00:11:10,942
And the test merge fails: an add/add
conflict in router/priority.py, a file that

153
00:11:10,982 --> 00:11:12,453
only you ever edited.

154
00:11:12,793 --> 00:11:18,127
The two facts of the root-cause box, measured:
the merge base is still 9a383e5, and

155
00:11:18,167 --> 00:11:20,134
the tip of main has one parent.

156
00:11:20,474 --> 00:11:22,615
Way out 1: move only what is new.

157
00:11:22,955 --> 00:11:26,602
Everything up to HEAD~1 is
already on main as content.

158
00:11:26,942 --> 00:11:30,580
One commit, one file, a clean
test merge, and a forced push.

159
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:37,741
Would a plain git rebase origin/main do
the same? It replays all four commits.

160
00:11:37,781 --> 00:11:41,044
Predict what happens to the
first, and to the second.

161
00:11:41,384 --> 00:11:46,333
Git dropped the first commit, "patch contents
already upstream", because replaying

162
00:11:46,373 --> 00:11:51,261
it changed nothing. The second conflicts,
because the squash already contains the

163
00:11:51,301 --> 00:11:55,763
third commit's fix of the same line.
A plain rebase relies on Git noticing

164
00:11:55,803 --> 00:11:59,109
duplicates. --onto tells Git
where the new work starts.

165
00:11:59,449 --> 00:12:03,998
Way out 2, in another copy: merge main
into the branch and resolve once.

166
00:12:07,338 --> 00:12:11,920
The branch has everything main has plus
the new word, so the branch side is the

167
00:12:11,960 --> 00:12:16,659
answer. The diff is correct afterwards.
The commit list still shows five commits,

168
00:12:16,699 --> 00:12:20,111
which does not matter if the
pull request is squash-merged.

169
00:12:20,451 --> 00:12:25,087
Way out 3, the prevention: a new branch
from main for every pull request.

170
00:12:25,427 --> 00:12:30,717
Create a second long-lived branch in your
practice repository, for example a release

171
00:12:30,757 --> 00:12:35,983
branch from an older commit, and open a
pull request from a branch that was created

172
00:12:36,023 --> 00:12:41,122
from main against that release branch. On
the page, read the commit count and the

173
00:12:41,162 --> 00:12:46,262
file count, and compare with your prediction
from git log --oneline <base>..HEAD.

174
00:12:46,602 --> 00:12:48,388
Then apply fix A on the platform:

175
00:12:48,728 --> 00:12:53,712
In the browser the same control is next
to the title: the edit control, then the

176
00:12:53,752 --> 00:12:58,799
base branch menu. Read the page again: the
documentation says some commits may be

177
00:12:58,839 --> 00:13:02,943
removed from the timeline and review
comments may become outdated.

178
00:13:02,983 --> 00:13:08,092
And note the prevention from the textbook:
gh pr create uses the default branch as

179
00:13:08,132 --> 00:13:09,851
base unless you pass --base.

180
00:13:11,691 --> 00:13:13,339
Five mistakes to watch for.

181
00:13:13,679 --> 00:13:17,159
Continuing on a branch after its
pull request was squash-merged.

182
00:13:17,199 --> 00:13:21,615
Root cause: the squash commit has no parent
link to the branch, so the merge base

183
00:13:21,655 --> 00:13:24,311
never moved and the old
commits are listed again.

184
00:13:24,651 --> 00:13:28,627
Fixing a wrong-base pull request
with a plain git rebase <base>.

185
00:13:28,667 --> 00:13:33,586
Root cause: it replays every commit, the
foreign ones included; --onto with the

186
00:13:33,626 --> 00:13:36,159
right upstream selects only your commits.

187
00:13:36,499 --> 00:13:40,621
Changing the base when the branch started
in the wrong place, or the reverse.

188
00:13:40,661 --> 00:13:45,000
Root cause: the two repairs answer different
questions: where the change belongs,

189
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:46,352
and where the work began.

190
00:13:46,692 --> 00:13:50,056
Opening a pull request
without looking at the range.

191
00:13:50,096 --> 00:13:55,228
Root cause: gh pr create uses the default
branch as base unless told otherwise.

192
00:13:55,568 --> 00:14:00,991
Trusting the "Files changed" tab to be
complete on a very large pull request.

193
00:14:01,031 --> 00:14:06,880
Root cause: display limits and .gitattributes
rules hide files that git diff --stat

194
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:08,157
base...head shows.

195
00:14:09,997 --> 00:14:15,668
Now, out of the lab. An ML platform team
maintains main and a release/2.4 branch for

196
00:14:15,708 --> 00:14:21,242
a customer deployment. An engineer fixes
a tokenizer bug for the release, branches

197
00:14:21,282 --> 00:14:25,797
from main by habit, and opens the
pull request against release/2.4.

198
00:14:25,837 --> 00:14:29,400
The page shows 212 commits
and several hundred files.

199
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:32,527
The reviewer refuses to
look at it, correctly.

200
00:14:32,867 --> 00:14:38,415
The engineer runs the diagnosis from this
video: the count of commits against each

201
00:14:38,455 --> 00:14:42,845
candidate base. One against main,
212 against the release branch.

202
00:14:42,885 --> 00:14:48,365
The base is right, the fix is needed in
the release, so the branch started in the

203
00:14:48,405 --> 00:14:54,022
wrong place: fix B. One git rebase --onto
origin/release/2.4 origin/main, one check

204
00:14:54,062 --> 00:14:59,678
of the range, one push with --force-with-lease,
and the pull request is one commit.

205
00:15:00,018 --> 00:15:05,060
The team adds two lines to its
contribution guide: run git log --oneline

206
00:15:05,100 --> 00:15:10,919
<base>..HEAD before opening a pull request,
and head branches are deleted on merge.

207
00:15:12,759 --> 00:15:17,156
Your turn. Do Lab 21.2, "A pull
request against the wrong base", in

208
00:15:17,196 --> 00:15:22,652
lab-manual/m21-pull-requests-forks.md.
Before you run the diagnosis, write down how

209
00:15:22,692 --> 00:15:27,354
many commits the pull request lists
and which of them are the author's.

210
00:15:27,394 --> 00:15:31,857
Before each repair, predict the
commit list and the diff afterwards.

211
00:15:32,197 --> 00:15:37,087
The challenge is Lab 21.3, "The
squash-then-reuse problem", in the same file.

212
00:15:37,127 --> 00:15:40,225
Predict the merge base
before you ask Git for it.

213
00:15:42,065 --> 00:15:45,320
Question 263 of the CTO question bank:

214
00:15:47,660 --> 00:15:52,057
Answer out loud. A strong answer starts
from what the commit list is, as a Git

215
00:15:52,097 --> 00:15:56,836
range, and then gives causes as statements
about the endpoints of that range, not as

216
00:15:56,876 --> 00:16:01,045
a list of anecdotes. For each cause it
names the command that confirms it.

217
00:16:01,085 --> 00:16:05,141
It treats squash-then-reuse with the
mechanism of the merge base, and it

218
00:16:05,181 --> 00:16:07,815
distinguishes the two
repairs for a wrong base.

219
00:16:09,655 --> 00:16:14,728
Let's land this. When a pull request shows
too much, you now ask one thing first:

220
00:16:14,768 --> 00:16:16,433
where does the range start?

221
00:16:16,773 --> 00:16:18,433
You should now be able to say:

222
00:16:18,773 --> 00:16:23,491
A pull request that shows too much has a
head with commits the base does not reach,

223
00:16:23,531 --> 00:16:25,670
or a base the work did not start from.

224
00:16:26,010 --> 00:16:31,186
The five documented causes are: reuse after
a squash merge, a wrong or changed base,

225
00:16:31,226 --> 00:16:35,408
a base that moved, rewritten history,
and truncated or hidden diffs.

226
00:16:35,748 --> 00:16:40,649
git merge-base and git rev-list
--count base..head test the first two.

227
00:16:40,989 --> 00:16:46,232
A wrong base is fixed by changing the base
or by git rebase --onto, depending on

228
00:16:46,272 --> 00:16:47,882
where the change belongs.

229
00:16:48,222 --> 00:16:52,821
After a squash merge the branch's commits
are not ancestors of the base; delete the

230
00:16:52,861 --> 00:16:54,330
branch and start from main.

231
00:16:56,170 --> 00:17:04,671
Read section 17.12 of Chapter 17. Do Exercise
21.6, "Five commits for a two-commit

232
00:17:04,711 --> 00:17:08,525
fix", in exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

233
00:17:08,865 --> 00:17:13,635
You can now name the cause of an oversized
pull request and prove it with one

234
00:17:13,675 --> 00:17:18,696
command. Practise with Lab 21.2. Next:
indirect merges and stacked pull requests.

235
00:17:18,736 --> 00:17:21,944
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

236
00:17:21,984 --> 00:17:23,444
See you in the next one.
